//@version=6
indicator(title="ATR MTF V2", overlay = true)
//CREDITS to HPotter for the orginal ATR-trailing-stop code. The guy trying
//to sell this as his own is a scammer lol.
//
//Ported to Pine v6 (2026-08-27) -- syntax-only (study->indicator,
//atr->ta.atr, max/min/round->math.*, tostring->str.tostring, typed
//input.*() instead of generic input()); no logic changed.
//
//Consolidated 2026-08-20 (v1.0, renamed from atr_trail_webhook.pine) --
//was two separate copies of this indicator on the same chart (Key
//Value/ATR Period pairs (2,2) and (2,300)), one per ATR period, so
//whichever period's trend flips FIRST could be used as the entry
//confirmation trigger. Consolidated into ONE indicator computing BOTH
//trail lines internally instead of running two full copies -- same
//"headless where it doesn't need to draw" philosophy as OBD_Reversal:
//buy/sell shape labels and bar coloring dropped entirely (were only ever
//visual sugar tied to a single trail line, meaningless with two), each
//line still plots its own trailing-stop for visual reference, and each
//fires its own atr_trail webhook (see the send-gate section below for
//exactly when, as of v1.1), tagged with which ATR period it came from
//(see the "atr_period" payload field below) so the Python side
//(v3/tradingview_bot/atr_store.py's AtrStore) can track both readings
//independently instead of one overwriting the other.
//
//Every statement below is deliberately kept to ONE line (no wrapped/
//hanging-indent expressions) even where that makes lines long -- Pine's
//block parsing is indentation-sensitive, and a wrapped multi-line
//expression typed through TradingView's Pine Editor let auto-indent
//stack on top of this file's own indentation, corrupting it (confirmed
//live 2026-08-20). One statement per line sidesteps that entirely: every
//line inside a block sits at the exact same single indent level, so
//auto-indent (which just repeats the previous line's level) can't drift.
//
//v1.2 (2026-09-01) -- added up to 4 higher-timeframe readings for BOTH
//periods, on top of the chart's own (native) timeframe. Each HTF is a
//menu-selectable input.timeframe() (renders as TradingView's own
//timeframe picker dropdown in the indicator's Inputs tab) instead of a
//hardcoded value, plus its own enable toggle so unused slots can be
//switched off without deleting them. Fetched via request.security()
//calling the SAME recursive compute_trail_full() used for the native
//timeframe -- request.security() runs a UDF's var/[1] recursion in its
//own independent per-(symbol,timeframe) context, so each HTF tracks its
//own trail/trend state correctly, no cross-contamination between HTFs or
//with the native line.
//
//Repaint note: HTF results are deliberately delayed by one HTF-closed-bar
//(compute_trail_confirmed()'s trailing [1] indexing) before being sent.
//Without that, request.security() on the currently-forming realtime bar
//returns the STILL-FORMING higher-timeframe value, which can change
//intrabar -- since alert.freq_once_per_bar_close gates on the chart's own
//(native) bar close, not the HTF's, that would risk firing a webhook on a
//value that flips back before the real HTF bar actually closes. Using the
//last CONFIRMED HTF bar avoids that at the cost of up to one HTF bar of
//latency on HTF flips -- consistent with this project's general bias
//toward avoiding false/premature signals (see v3/v4 retest-staleness
//fixes). The native (chart-timeframe) line is untouched by this and keeps
//its original zero-added-latency behavior.
src = close

webhook_secret = input.string("", title="Webhook Secret")
// Heartbeat interval (2026-08-24, v1.1) -- was firing every single bar
// close unconditionally, which meant one webhook per 3-min bar forever
// even when trend never changed (that volume is what prompted this).
// Now fires ONLY on an actual trend flip OR every N bars (this input),
// whichever comes first -- user's own choice, "for every 10 candles plus
// a flip." The periodic heartbeat is still needed even though it's not a
// real signal: it's the only way to tell "trend hasn't flipped" apart
// from "the whole pipeline silently died," which happened for real this
// week (tv_bridge.receiver was down for hours with nothing to show it
// except total silence). event_time_N below still only updates on a
// genuine flip either way, so a heartbeat resend never looks like a new
// flip on the Python side. Heartbeat cadence is tied to the CHART's own
// bar_index for every line below, HTFs included -- its whole job is
// "prove the pipeline is alive," not "resend on every HTF close," so it
// deliberately stays decoupled from each line's own timeframe.
heartbeat_bars = input.int(10, title="Heartbeat every N bars", minval=1)

// --- Line 1 (fast, by default) ---
keyvalue_1 = input.float(2, title = "Line 1 Key Value", step = .5, group = "Line 1")
atrperiod_1 = input.int(2, title = "Line 1 ATR Period", group = "Line 1")

// --- Line 2 (slow, by default) ---
keyvalue_2 = input.float(2, title = "Line 2 Key Value", step = .5, group = "Line 2")
atrperiod_2 = input.int(300, title = "Line 2 ATR Period", group = "Line 2")

// --- Higher timeframes (v1.2) -- each is a menu-selectable timeframe
// picker with its own on/off switch, applied to BOTH periods above. ---
enable_htf1 = input.bool(true, title = "Enable HTF 1", group = "Higher Timeframes")
htf1 = input.timeframe("15", title = "HTF 1", group = "Higher Timeframes")
enable_htf2 = input.bool(true, title = "Enable HTF 2", group = "Higher Timeframes")
htf2 = input.timeframe("60", title = "HTF 2", group = "Higher Timeframes")
enable_htf3 = input.bool(true, title = "Enable HTF 3", group = "Higher Timeframes")
htf3 = input.timeframe("240", title = "HTF 3", group = "Higher Timeframes")
enable_htf4 = input.bool(true, title = "Enable HTF 4", group = "Higher Timeframes")
htf4 = input.timeframe("D", title = "HTF 4", group = "Higher Timeframes")
show_htf_plots = input.bool(false, title = "Plot HTF trail lines on chart", group = "Higher Timeframes")

bar_time_sec = math.round(time / 1000)

//Function to compute the ATR trailing stop + trend (1/-1) for one
//(keyvalue, atrperiod) pair -- same logic the original single-line
//script used (ratcheted trailing stop, trend flips on a cross), just
//written as flat ternary chains instead of nested multi-line iff() calls
//so every line stays at one indent level (see module header). Also
//returns this pair's own flip flag + flip event time (moved in here in
//v1.2 so the SAME function can be reused verbatim for HTF readings via
//request.security() below -- time/close inside resolve to whichever
//context the function is evaluated in, native or requested).
compute_trail_full(keyvalue, atrperiod) =>
    xATR = ta.atr(atrperiod)
    nLoss = keyvalue * xATR
    var float trail = 0.0
    above_prev = src > nz(trail[1], 0) and src[1] > nz(trail[1], 0)
    below_prev = src < nz(trail[1], 0) and src[1] < nz(trail[1], 0)
    still_above = src > nz(trail[1], 0)
    trail := above_prev ? math.max(nz(trail[1]), src - nLoss) : below_prev ? math.min(nz(trail[1]), src + nLoss) : (still_above ? src - nLoss : src + nLoss)
    var int trend = 0
    crossed_up = src[1] < nz(trail[1], 0) and src > nz(trail[1], 0)
    crossed_dn = src[1] > nz(trail[1], 0) and src < nz(trail[1], 0)
    trend := crossed_up ? 1 : crossed_dn ? -1 : nz(trend[1], 0)
    var float event_time_ms = na
    flipped = trend != nz(trend[1], 0)
    if flipped
        event_time_ms := time
    event_time_sec = na(event_time_ms) ? 0 : math.round(event_time_ms / 1000)
    [trail, trend, event_time_sec, flipped]

//HTF-only wrapper: same computation, but returns the previous (already
//CONFIRMED) bar's values instead of the current, still-forming one -- see
//the v1.2 repaint note in the module header for why. Only used inside the
//request.security() calls below; the native lines call compute_trail_full()
//directly and keep their original zero-added-latency behavior.
compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue, atrperiod) =>
    [trail, trend, event_time_sec, flipped] = compute_trail_full(keyvalue, atrperiod)
    [trail[1], trend[1], event_time_sec[1], flipped[1]]

[trail_1, trend_1, event_time_1_sec, flipped_1] = compute_trail_full(keyvalue_1, atrperiod_1)
[trail_2, trend_2, event_time_2_sec, flipped_2] = compute_trail_full(keyvalue_2, atrperiod_2)

[trail_1_htf1, trend_1_htf1, event_time_1_htf1_sec, flipped_1_htf1] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf1, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_1, atrperiod_1), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_2_htf1, trend_2_htf1, event_time_2_htf1_sec, flipped_2_htf1] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf1, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_2, atrperiod_2), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_1_htf2, trend_1_htf2, event_time_1_htf2_sec, flipped_1_htf2] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf2, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_1, atrperiod_1), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_2_htf2, trend_2_htf2, event_time_2_htf2_sec, flipped_2_htf2] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf2, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_2, atrperiod_2), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_1_htf3, trend_1_htf3, event_time_1_htf3_sec, flipped_1_htf3] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf3, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_1, atrperiod_1), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_2_htf3, trend_2_htf3, event_time_2_htf3_sec, flipped_2_htf3] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf3, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_2, atrperiod_2), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_1_htf4, trend_1_htf4, event_time_1_htf4_sec, flipped_1_htf4] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf4, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_1, atrperiod_1), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
[trail_2_htf4, trend_2_htf4, event_time_2_htf4_sec, flipped_2_htf4] = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, htf4, compute_trail_confirmed(keyvalue_2, atrperiod_2), lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

line1_color = input.color(color.gray, title = "Line 1 Color", group = "Line 1")
line2_color = input.color(color.gray, title = "Line 2 Color", group = "Line 2")

//v1.2 cut-on-flip: plotting trail_N directly as one continuous series
//draws a connecting diagonal straight through the flip bar, from the old
//trend's last value to the new trend's first (usually a big jump, since
//trail resets close to price right after a flip) -- reads as the line
//"sliding across" instead of cutting.
//
//First attempt at this (splitting into an up-only/down-only series with
//na on the other side, still using plot.style_line) did NOT fix it --
//plot.style_line is documented to bridge straight across na gaps with a
//connecting line, same as if the na wasn't there at all, so the diagonal
//kept showing. The actual fix (confirmed against a reference Supertrend
//script that does the same up/down-cut plot, v4 study("Supertrend")) is
//plot.style_linebr -- "line with breaks": identical to style_line except
//it genuinely breaks/discontinues the line at na instead of bridging it.
//Combined with the up/down split below, the up series now really ends at
//the last uptrend bar and the down series starts fresh at the flip bar,
//with nothing drawn connecting them -- same color as before per line,
//just cut instead of joined.
trail_1_up = trend_1 == 1 ? trail_1 : na
trail_1_dn = trend_1 == -1 ? trail_1 : na
trail_2_up = trend_2 == 1 ? trail_2 : na
trail_2_dn = trend_2 == -1 ? trail_2 : na

plot(trail_1_up, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_dn, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_up, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_dn, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)

// HTF trail plots are OFF by default (show_htf_plots) -- 16 extra lines
// on top of the native 4 is chart clutter most of the time, and this
// bot's own conventions favor keeping indicators lightweight (see
// MT5-side equivalents). Same up/down split + na-hide technique as
// above, gated by show_htf_plots too so both are na (nothing drawn) when
// off.
trail_1_htf1_up = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf1 == 1 ? trail_1_htf1 : na
trail_1_htf1_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf1 == -1 ? trail_1_htf1 : na
trail_2_htf1_up = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf1 == 1 ? trail_2_htf1 : na
trail_2_htf1_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf1 == -1 ? trail_2_htf1 : na
trail_1_htf2_up = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf2 == 1 ? trail_1_htf2 : na
trail_1_htf2_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf2 == -1 ? trail_1_htf2 : na
trail_2_htf2_up = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf2 == 1 ? trail_2_htf2 : na
trail_2_htf2_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf2 == -1 ? trail_2_htf2 : na
trail_1_htf3_up = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf3 == 1 ? trail_1_htf3 : na
trail_1_htf3_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf3 == -1 ? trail_1_htf3 : na
trail_2_htf3_up = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf3 == 1 ? trail_2_htf3 : na
trail_2_htf3_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf3 == -1 ? trail_2_htf3 : na
trail_1_htf4_up = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf4 == 1 ? trail_1_htf4 : na
trail_1_htf4_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_1_htf4 == -1 ? trail_1_htf4 : na
trail_2_htf4_up = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf4 == 1 ? trail_2_htf4 : na
trail_2_htf4_dn = show_htf_plots and trend_2_htf4 == -1 ? trail_2_htf4 : na

plot(trail_1_htf1_up, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF1 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf1_dn, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF1 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf1_up, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF1 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf1_dn, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF1 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf2_up, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF2 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf2_dn, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF2 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf2_up, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF2 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf2_dn, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF2 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf3_up, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF3 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf3_dn, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF3 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf3_up, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF3 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf3_dn, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF3 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf4_up, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF4 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_1_htf4_dn, color = line1_color, title = "Line 1 Trailing Stop HTF4 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf4_up, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF4 (Up)", style = plot.style_linebr)
plot(trail_2_htf4_dn, color = line2_color, title = "Line 2 Trailing Stop HTF4 (Down)", style = plot.style_linebr)

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//Webhook: fires per line, with that line's own trail/trend state --
//mirrors atr_bridge.ATRSnapshot's own shape (trail_stop/trend/event_time),
//plus a new "atr_period" field (this line's own ATR Period input,
//stringified) identifying WHICH period this particular reading came from
//-- AtrStore keys on symbol|timeframe|atr_period now, not just
//symbol|timeframe, specifically so two periods for the same
//symbol+timeframe don't overwrite each other. event_time_N only updates
//on an actual trend flip (matching the original script's own buy-or-sell
//gate, computed here directly from trend != trend[1] instead of separate
//buy/sell variables since those got dropped along with the shape labels).
//
//v1.1 (2026-08-24): only actually SENDS on a flip or every heartbeat_bars
//bars, not every bar close -- see heartbeat_bars input above for why the
//heartbeat half still matters. flipped_N drives the send gate;
//event_time_N stays flip-only regardless of the gate, so a heartbeat
//resend of an unchanged trend carries the SAME event_time as the flip
//that started it, not a fresh one -- the Python side can always tell
//"still the same trend since X" from "just flipped."
//
//v1.2 (2026-09-01): payload building + the send-gate/alert() call are now
//one shared function (send_atr_alert()) instead of being duplicated per
//line, since there are 10 lines total (native x2 + 4 HTFs x2) instead of
//2. Each HTF payload's "timeframe" field carries that HTF's own
//input.timeframe() string (e.g. "60", "D") instead of the chart's
//timeframe.period, so the Python side (AtrStore) can key/store each HTF
//reading independently from the native one and from each other.
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------{
send_atr_alert(secret, sym, tf_label, atrperiod, trail, trend, event_time_sec, bartime_sec, do_send) =>
    payload = '{"secret":"' + secret + '","type":"atr_trail","symbol":"' + sym + '","timeframe":"' + tf_label + '","atr_period":"' + str.tostring(atrperiod, '#') + '","trail_stop":' + str.tostring(trail) + ',"trend":' + str.tostring(trend, '#') + ',"event_time":' + str.tostring(event_time_sec, '#') + ',"bar_time":' + str.tostring(bartime_sec, '#') + '}'
    if do_send
        alert(payload, alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

send_1 = flipped_1 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, timeframe.period, atrperiod_1, trail_1, trend_1, event_time_1_sec, bar_time_sec, send_1)

send_2 = flipped_2 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, timeframe.period, atrperiod_2, trail_2, trend_2, event_time_2_sec, bar_time_sec, send_2)

send_1_htf1 = enable_htf1 and (flipped_1_htf1 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf1, atrperiod_1, trail_1_htf1, trend_1_htf1, event_time_1_htf1_sec, bar_time_sec, send_1_htf1)

send_2_htf1 = enable_htf1 and (flipped_2_htf1 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf1, atrperiod_2, trail_2_htf1, trend_2_htf1, event_time_2_htf1_sec, bar_time_sec, send_2_htf1)

send_1_htf2 = enable_htf2 and (flipped_1_htf2 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf2, atrperiod_1, trail_1_htf2, trend_1_htf2, event_time_1_htf2_sec, bar_time_sec, send_1_htf2)

send_2_htf2 = enable_htf2 and (flipped_2_htf2 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf2, atrperiod_2, trail_2_htf2, trend_2_htf2, event_time_2_htf2_sec, bar_time_sec, send_2_htf2)

send_1_htf3 = enable_htf3 and (flipped_1_htf3 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf3, atrperiod_1, trail_1_htf3, trend_1_htf3, event_time_1_htf3_sec, bar_time_sec, send_1_htf3)

send_2_htf3 = enable_htf3 and (flipped_2_htf3 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf3, atrperiod_2, trail_2_htf3, trend_2_htf3, event_time_2_htf3_sec, bar_time_sec, send_2_htf3)

send_1_htf4 = enable_htf4 and (flipped_1_htf4 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf4, atrperiod_1, trail_1_htf4, trend_1_htf4, event_time_1_htf4_sec, bar_time_sec, send_1_htf4)

send_2_htf4 = enable_htf4 and (flipped_2_htf4 or bar_index % heartbeat_bars == 0)
send_atr_alert(webhook_secret, syminfo.ticker, htf4, atrperiod_2, trail_2_htf4, trend_2_htf4, event_time_2_htf4_sec, bar_time_sec, send_2_htf4)

//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------}
//NOT plotting trend_1/trend_2 (deliberately, 2026-08-20) -- the original
//script's own comment claimed a transparent -1/0/1 plot is "negligible
//against price scale," but that's wrong: autoscale fits the WHOLE
//visible history's min/max across every plot on the price scale
//regardless of that plot's own color/transparency, so a -1..1 range
//sitting next to real price (thousands) forces the y-axis to span from
//-1 up to full price -- confirmed live as the actual cause of "chart
//gets squeezed on reset/load." Also confirmed unnecessary: tv_scraper's
//own atr_trend_tracker.py derives trend from trail_stop + live Close
//directly, specifically INSTEAD OF needing this plot (a separate, older
//fix for missed-flip polling gaps) -- nothing downstream reads it. Same
//reasoning applies to every HTF trend_*_htf* value -- none of them are
//plotted either.
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------{
alertcondition(trend_1 != nz(trend_1[1], 0), 'Line 1 Trend Flip', 'OBD_ATR Line 1 trend flipped')
alertcondition(trend_2 != nz(trend_2[1], 0), 'Line 2 Trend Flip', 'OBD_ATR Line 2 trend flipped')

// NOTE: as with OBD_Reversal, these alertcondition() entries are for the
// alert dialog's dropdown discoverability only -- the actual TradingView
// alert must use "Any alert() function call" as its condition, or none of
// the atr_payload_* JSON (native or HTF) ever gets sent. HTF flips
// deliberately don't get their own alertcondition() entries here -- same
// reason: purely cosmetic dropdown sugar, unused once the alert is
// actually configured with "Any alert() function call".
